  "adderallPageTitle": "Quitting Adderall",
  "adderallHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "adderallHeaderNotStarted": "Ready to Start Your Recovery?",
  "adderallSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone as your brain heals",
  "adderallSubtitleNotStarted": "See what happens when you quit Adderall and start your journey to recovery",
  "adderallMilestone1Title": "First day without misuse",
  "adderallMilestone1Description": "The first day after stopping stimulant misuse can bring a crash: exhaustion, low mood and a bigger appetite. Give yourself rest and support.",
  "adderallMilestone3Title": "The crash can hit hard",
  "adderallMilestone3Description": "Day three can be miserable: tired, irritable and struggling to focus. If your mood gets dangerously low, reach out for urgent help.",
  "adderallMilestone7Title": "A week of change",
  "adderallMilestone7Description": "Seven days without misusing Adderall. The early crash may be easing, though sleep and motivation can take longer.",
  "adderallMilestone14Title": "Two weeks clear",
  "adderallMilestone14Description": "Two weeks. Mood and sleep may be finding a steadier rhythm after stimulant misuse. You're getting more practice with the difficult bits.",
  "adderallMilestone30Title": "A month without misuse",
  "adderallMilestone30Description": "Thirty days. Appetite, sleep and energy may be improving. Focus can take time, and prescribed ADHD care is still valid care.",
  "adderallMilestone60Title": "Two months, more clarity",
  "adderallMilestone60Description": "Two months without misuse. Memory, mood and everyday motivation may be improving as recovery continues.",
  "adderallMilestone90Title": "Three months",
  "adderallMilestone90Description": "Three months without misusing Adderall. That's a lot of mornings and difficult afternoons handled differently.",
  "adderallMilestone180Title": "Half a year clear",
  "adderallMilestone180Description": "Six months. Attention and mood can keep improving in recovery. You've also built six months of experience without the old pattern.",
  "adderallMilestone365Title": "A full year",
  "adderallMilestone365Description": "One year without Adderall misuse. You've spent a full year changing the habits around it.",
  "benzoPageTitle": "Benzodiazepines",
  "benzoHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "benzoHeaderNotStarted": "Benefits of Quitting Benzodiazepines",
  "benzoSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone",
  "benzoSubtitleNotStarted": "See what happens when you quit",
  "benzoMilestone7Title": "One week: stay supported",
  "benzoMilestone7Description": "Benzodiazepine withdrawal can be dangerous, especially after abrupt stopping. Symptoms may only just be starting with longer-acting medicines; tapering needs medical guidance.",
  "benzoMilestone14Title": "Two weeks after taper",
  "benzoMilestone14Description": "Two weeks clear. Some people notice better sleep after a gradual taper; others still have withdrawal symptoms. Both deserve care and patience.",
  "benzoMilestone60Title": "Eight weeks, clearer days",
  "benzoMilestone60Description": "In a study of older long-term users, people who completed tapering showed gains in memory, attention and balance by eight weeks. Your pace may differ.",
  "benzoMilestone90Title": "Three months, less fog",
  "benzoMilestone90Description": "Research has found faster thinking and less sedation in some people after tapering off benzodiazepines. Three months is a meaningful stretch, whatever your own pace.",
  "benzoMilestone180Title": "Six months of recovery",
  "benzoMilestone180Description": "Half a year. Memory and attention can improve after long-term use, and some changes continue beyond six months.",
  "benzoMilestone365Title": "A year after benzos",
  "benzoMilestone365Description": "One year. Sleep, thinking and wellbeing may keep improving, though withdrawal can be prolonged for some.",
  "benzoMilestone540Title": "Eighteen months clear",
  "benzoMilestone540Description": "A year and a half. Recovery doesn't always follow a straight line. You've given it serious time, and any continuing symptoms still deserve support.",
  "benzoMilestone730Title": "Two years",
  "benzoMilestone730Description": "Two years after benzodiazepines. It's a major stretch of recovery, even if parts of it have been anything but easy.",
  "benzoMilestone1095Title": "Three years of progress",
  "benzoMilestone1095Description": "Three years. Sleep, mood and concentration may be different now, but nobody gets the same recovery schedule.",
  "benzoMilestone1460Title": "Four years of work",
  "benzoMilestone1460Description": "Four years after benzos. That's a long time spent managing recovery, keeping routines and finding what helps.",
  "benzoMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "benzoMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade. Benzodiazepine recovery can be complicated, which makes five years of follow-through all the more worth celebrating.",
  "addictionMeth": "Meth",
  "addictionBenzos": "Benzos",
  "methPageTitle": "Meth Recovery",
  "methHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "methHeaderNotStarted": "Drop the Glass",
  "methSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone",
  "methSubtitleNotStarted": "Freedom is within your grasp",
  "methMilestone1Title": "The crash",
  "methMilestone1Description": "Day one without meth can mean heavy fatigue, low mood and a lot of sleep. Your body is adjusting. Let people support you.",
  "methMilestone3Title": "Three days",
  "methMilestone3Description": "Cravings and mood swings can be fierce in the first days. Three days clear is something to recognise, especially if it's been a slog.",
  "methMilestone7Title": "A week without meth",
  "methMilestone7Description": "Seven days clear. The early crash may be easing, even if cravings and sleep are still giving you grief.",
  "methMilestone30Title": "One month clear",
  "methMilestone30Description": "Thirty days without meth. Energy and mood may be starting to settle. Recovery isn't linear, and that doesn't undo the work.",
  "methMilestone90Title": "Three months of recovery",
  "methMilestone90Description": "Three months. Attention, memory and decision-making can improve with abstinence. You've also had time to practise different routines.",
  "methMilestone180Title": "Six months, still clear",
  "methMilestone180Description": "Half a year without meth. Heart health can improve after stopping, especially with treatment when needed.",
  "methMilestone365Title": "One year without meth",
  "methMilestone365Description": "A full year. The brain's reward system can recover over sustained abstinence, and you've given it twelve months without renewed exposure.",
  "methMilestone420Title": "Fourteen months on",
  "methMilestone420Description": "Fourteen months. Brain-imaging studies have found some dopamine-related recovery after long abstinence.",
  "methMilestone730Title": "Two years",
  "methMilestone730Description": "Two years without meth. Thinking and mood can keep improving, and your life has had two years to grow around different choices.",
  "addictionNitrousOxide": "Nitrous Oxide",
  "nitrousOxidePageTitle": "Nitrous Oxide",
  "nitrousOxideHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "nitrousOxideHeaderNotStarted": "Ready to Start?",
  "nitrousOxideSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone",
  "nitrousOxideSubtitleNotStarted": "See what positive changes await you",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone1Title": "First day without nitrous",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone1Description": "Nitrous leaves the body quickly, but nerve problems from vitamin B12 inactivation can last. Numbness, weakness or trouble walking needs urgent assessment.",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone3Title": "Three days clear",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone3Description": "Three days without nitrous. Cravings or habits may still pull at you. If you've had tingling or weakness, don't wait for it to fix itself.",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone7Title": "One week, no new exposure",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone7Description": "Seven days without more nitrous-related B12 inactivation. Nerve recovery may need proper B12 treatment and medical follow-up.",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone14Title": "Two weeks clear",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone14Description": "Two weeks. If nitrous affected your nerves, recovery can take time even with treatment. Staying off it prevents further exposure.",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone30Title": "A month without nitrous",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone30Description": "Thirty days without nitrous. B12-related symptoms may improve after treatment, but some nerve damage can persist. Keep up medical follow-up.",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone90Title": "Three months of recovery",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone90Description": "Three months. Nerve recovery can continue over time with cessation and treatment. Persistent weakness or numbness still matters.",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone365Title": "A year clear",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone365Description": "A full year without nitrous oxide. You've avoided another year of B12 inactivation. How much nerve function returns varies, but this is a big step.",
  "addictionCocaine": "Cocaine",
  "addictionAdderall": "Adderall",
  "cocainePageTitle": "Cocaine Recovery",
  "cocaineHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "cocaineHeaderNotStarted": "Ready to Start?",
  "cocaineSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone",
  "cocaineSubtitleNotStarted": "See what positive changes await you",
  "cocaineMilestone1Title": "First day without cocaine",
  "cocaineMilestone1Description": "The first day can bring exhaustion, low mood and cravings. Your heart is no longer getting another hit of cocaine's acute strain.",
  "cocaineMilestone3Title": "The tough early days",
  "cocaineMilestone3Description": "Day three can bring strong cravings, irritability and a proper energy crash. If your mood becomes unsafe, get help straight away.",
  "cocaineMilestone7Title": "A week clear",
  "cocaineMilestone7Description": "Seven days without cocaine. The initial crash may be easing, while sleep and cravings can take longer to settle.",
  "cocaineMilestone14Title": "Two weeks without it",
