  "nicotinePouchesMilestone1Title": "First day pouch-free",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone1Title": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 1 title"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone1Description": "Nicotine withdrawal can begin within hours. Irritability, cravings or poor focus may show up. One full day without a pouch is a start.",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone1Description": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 1 description"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone3Title": "The cravings are loud",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone3Title": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 3 title"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone3Description": "Nicotine withdrawal often peaks around day three. You're in a rough bit, but cravings tend to ease over time.",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone3Description": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 3 description"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesReferenceDay1": "Day One: Nicotine Withdrawal Begins\n\nSource: McLaughlin, Dani & De Biasi — Nicotine Withdrawal\n\nA review of withdrawal from chronic use of nicotine-containing products reports that the withdrawal syndrome can begin 4–24 hours after stopping. That makes the first pouch-free day a real physiological milestone.\n\nWhat happens next\nSymptoms typically peak around day 3 and then taper over the following 3–4 weeks. Withdrawal intensity varies with how nicotine was consumed, so the experience can differ from person to person.",
  "@nicotinePouchesReferenceDay1": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches day 1 detailed reference"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesReferenceDay3": "Day Three: Peak Nicotine Withdrawal\n\nSource: McLaughlin, Dani & De Biasi — Nicotine Withdrawal\n\nThe review places the typical nicotine-withdrawal peak at about the third day after stopping chronic nicotine use.\n\nThe curve from here\nAfter the peak, symptoms usually taper over the following 3–4 weeks. The review also notes that withdrawal severity varies with how nicotine was consumed, so the exact intensity is individual.\n\nDay three is a hard-earned milestone: you've reached the top of the typical early withdrawal curve.",
  "@nicotinePouchesReferenceDay3": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches day 3 detailed reference"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone7Title": "A week without pouches",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone7Title": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 7 title"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone7Description": "Seven days pouch-free. You're past the typical early nicotine-withdrawal peak, even if urges keep popping up.",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone7Description": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 7 description"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone14Title": "Two weeks clear",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone14Title": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 14 title"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone14Description": "Two weeks without a pouch. Nicotine withdrawal usually eases over the first few weeks. You're well into that stretch.",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone14Description": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 14 description"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesReferenceDay7": "One Week: Withdrawal Is Easing\n\nSource: McLaughlin, Dani & De Biasi — Nicotine Withdrawal\n\nThe review reports that withdrawal from chronic use of nicotine-containing products begins 4–24 hours after stopping, peaks around day 3, then tapers over the following 3–4 weeks.\n\nA real one-week win\nAt one week, you’re several days past the typical peak and into the taper phase. The review also notes that withdrawal severity varies with how nicotine was consumed, so intensity differs between people.\n\nOne week pouch-free means the steepest part of the typical early withdrawal curve is behind you.",
  "@nicotinePouchesReferenceDay7": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches day 7 detailed reference"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesReferenceDay14": "Two Weeks: Withdrawal Keeps Easing\n\nSource: McLaughlin, Dani & De Biasi — Nicotine Withdrawal\n\nThe review places the typical nicotine-withdrawal peak around day 3 and says symptoms taper over the following 3–4 weeks.\n\nWell into the taper\nAt two weeks, you’re well into that post-peak taper window. The review covers chronic use of nicotine-containing products and notes that withdrawal severity varies with how nicotine was consumed.\n\nTwo weeks pouch-free is a concrete checkpoint on the downward curve: the typical withdrawal syndrome is continuing to ease.",
  "@nicotinePouchesReferenceDay14": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches day 14 detailed reference"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone730Title": "Two years, no pouches",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone730Title": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 730 title"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone730Description": "Two years free of nicotine pouches. You haven't reinforced that habit for 730 days.",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone730Description": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 730 description"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesReferenceDay730": "Two Years: Pouch-Free Power\n\nSource: Rungraungrayabkul et al. (2024), systematic review of nicotine-pouch oral health\n\nThe review found only three human studies with 190 participants, all judged at high risk of bias. It did not establish a two-year recovery threshold after stopping.\n\nTwo years pouch-free is still a major sustained-change milestone. This entry celebrates that achievement without inventing a medical timeline.",
  "@nicotinePouchesReferenceDay730": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches reference content for day 730"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone1095Title": "Three years",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone1095Title": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 1095 title"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone1095Description": "Three years pouch-free. Long-term research is still developing; three years of keeping the change is already plenty to celebrate.",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone1095Description": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 1095 description"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesReferenceDay1095": "Three Years: Freedom Holds\n\nSource: CDC — Nicotine Pouches\n\nCDC says more research is needed to understand the short- and long-term health effects of nicotine pouches. It does not identify a specific medical change at three years after stopping.\n\nThree years pouch-free is worth celebrating for what it is: three years of sustained freedom from pouch nicotine.",
  "@nicotinePouchesReferenceDay1095": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches reference content for day 1095"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone1460Title": "Four years without them",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone1460Title": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 1460 title"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone1460Description": "Four years. No pouch habit taking up a corner of your day. You've kept it that way for a long time.",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone1460Description": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 1460 description"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesReferenceDay1460": "Four Years: Built to Last\n\nSource: Rungraungrayabkul et al. (2024), systematic review of nicotine-pouch oral health\n\nThe review concludes that evidence on short- and long-term oral-health effects remains limited and more research is needed. It does not support a universal four-year recovery event.\n\nFour years pouch-free is a milestone in sustained change, without needing an unsupported medical claim.",
  "@nicotinePouchesReferenceDay1460": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches reference content for day 1460"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone1825Title": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 1825 title"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade pouch-free. Five years of not going back to nicotine pouches. Fair play, that's a serious anniversary.",
  "@nicotinePouchesMilestone1825Description": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches milestone day 1825 description"
  },
  "nicotinePouchesReferenceDay1825": "Five Years: Five-Year Victory\n\nSource: CDC — Nicotine Pouches\n\nCDC notes that nicotine pouches are relatively new and their long-term health effects are still being studied. Current evidence does not establish a specific five-year recovery event.\n\nFive years pouch-free is a huge achievement on its own: five years of sustained change.",
  "@nicotinePouchesReferenceDay1825": {
    "description": "Nicotine pouches reference content for day 1825"
  },
