  },
  "customMilestone365Title": "A full year",
  "@customMilestone365Title": {
    "description": "Custom milestone day 365 title"
  },
  "customMilestone365Description": "You've made room for a different habit through a full year of birthdays, seasons, workdays and days off.",
  "@customMilestone365Description": {
    "description": "Custom milestone day 365 description"
  },
  "customMilestone730Title": "Two years",
  "@customMilestone730Title": {
    "description": "Custom milestone day 730 title"
  },
  "customMilestone730Description": "Two years. The routines and support that got you started have carried you through another year.",
  "@customMilestone730Description": {
    "description": "Custom milestone day 730 description"
  },
  "customMilestone1095Title": "Three years, still going",
  "customMilestone1095Description": "Three years. The old habit has had fewer and fewer chances to run your day. You've built something that lasts.",
  "customMilestone1460Title": "Four years",
  "customMilestone1460Description": "Four years of sustained work on the habit you chose to change. You can be proud of keeping it going.",
  "customMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "customMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade of sticking with the change you chose. Whatever you were quitting, five years is a seriously big deal.",
  "customReferenceDay1095": "Three Years of Recovery: Deep Roots\n\nSource: NIDA, Drugs, Brains, and Behavior: The Science of Addiction\n\nNIDA describes substance use disorders as treatable and recovery as stopping drug use and resuming a productive life. It does not set a universal three-year biological recovery deadline across substances.\n\nThree years is a durable achievement in its own right. The routines, support, and choices that carried you here have had years to take root.",
  "customReferenceDay1460": "Four Years of Recovery: Lasting Change\n\nSource: NIDA, Drugs, Brains, and Behavior: The Science of Addiction\n\nNIDA presents addiction as a treatable condition that can be managed successfully. It does not define a universal four-year health endpoint for every substance or person.\n\nFour years is lasting change made visible through consistency. Keep protecting the habits, relationships, and support that make recovery sustainable.",
  "customReferenceDay1825": "Five Years of Recovery: Half a Decade\n\nSource: NIDA, Drugs, Brains, and Behavior: The Science of Addiction\n\nNIDA supports evidence-based treatment and recovery but does not claim that every person reaches the same biological state at five years.\n\nFive years is half a decade of sustained progress. Celebrate the life, skills, and support network you have built and keep investing in what works.",
  "addictionSsri": "SSRIs",
