  "snriPageTitle": "SNRI Recovery",
  "snriHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "snriHeaderNotStarted": "Benefits of Tapering Off SNRIs",
  "snriSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone",
  "snriSubtitleNotStarted": "See what happens when you taper off",
  "snriMilestone3Title": "Day three: get support",
  "snriMilestone3Description": "SNRI withdrawal, especially from venlafaxine, can start quickly. Dizziness, nausea, insomnia and 'brain zaps' can be rough. Speak to your prescriber about symptoms.",
  "snriMilestone7Title": "One week into the change",
  "snriMilestone7Description": "Seven days after stopping. Serotonin and noradrenaline pathways are adjusting, and symptoms may still be intense. Help is available; you needn't just endure it.",
  "snriMilestone14Title": "Two weeks, still adjusting",
  "snriMilestone14Description": "Two weeks. Physical discontinuation symptoms may be easing, but the timeline differs from person to person. Keep your clinician in the loop.",
  "snriMilestone30Title": "A month after SNRI taper",
  "snriMilestone30Description": "Thirty days since stopping. Sleep, energy and mood might be more settled, or not yet.",
  "snriMilestone90Title": "Three months, worth checking",
  "snriMilestone90Description": "Three months after stopping. Many acute symptoms ease within weeks, but longer withdrawal does happen. Check in about anything persistent or worsening.",
  "snriMilestone180Title": "Half a year on",
  "snriMilestone180Description": "Six months after stopping. Your experience may be smoother now, or you may still need support. Recovery takes different amounts of time for different people.",
  "snriMilestone365Title": "One year after stopping",
  "snriMilestone365Description": "A full year since your SNRI taper. If you're well, that's significant. Keep treating your mental health as something worth looking after.",
  "snriMilestone730Title": "Two years",
  "snriMilestone730Description": "Two years since stopping your SNRI. How you're feeling matters more than the date.",
  "snriReferenceDay730": "Two Years After SNRIs: A Progress Marker\n\nSource: Royal College of Psychiatrists — Stopping antidepressants\n\nThe Royal College advises that antidepressants should not usually be stopped suddenly. Tapering should be adapted to the individual, and higher-withdrawal-risk medicines such as duloxetine or venlafaxine may need slower reductions over months or longer.\n\nThe guidance does not define a two-year biological recovery threshold after stopping an SNRI. This anniversary celebrates sustained progress without claiming that a specific brain or body process completes today.",
  "snriMilestone1095Title": "Three years, steady",
  "snriMilestone1095Description": "Three years. You've had a lot of time to find what supports your wellbeing.",
  "snriReferenceDay1095": "Three Years After SNRIs: Progress That Lasts\n\nSource: Royal College of Psychiatrists — Stopping antidepressants\n\nWithdrawal experiences differ from person to person. Tapering speed should reflect the individual, treatment duration, prior withdrawal symptoms, and the medicine being taken.\n\nNo clinical guidance establishes three years as a universal SNRI recovery deadline. This milestone recognises the progress and stability maintained over three years.",
  "snriMilestone1460Title": "Four years of looking after you",
  "snriMilestone1460Description": "Four years after the taper. Keep checking how you're doing and getting the care you need.",
  "snriReferenceDay1460": "Four Years After SNRIs: Long-Term Progress\n\nSource: Royal College of Psychiatrists — Stopping antidepressants\n\nThe Royal College describes antidepressant withdrawal as variable and recommends gradual, individualised dose reductions. Duloxetine and venlafaxine are among medicines with a higher risk of withdrawal symptoms.\n\nThat evidence does not create a four-year neurological milestone. Today celebrates four years of sustained progress, not a claim that everyone follows the same biological timetable.",
  "snriMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "snriMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade since stopping your SNRI. If that's worked well for you, it's a big personal milestone.",
  "snriReferenceDay1825": "Five Years After SNRIs: Five-Year Milestone\n\nSource: Royal College of Psychiatrists — Stopping antidepressants\n\nThe Royal College guidance focuses on safe, individualised tapering and responding to withdrawal symptoms rather than assigning one fixed recovery schedule to everyone.\n\nThere is no evidence-based five-year SNRI recovery threshold in that guidance. Five years is still a meaningful personal anniversary and a strong marker of long-term progress.",
  "settingsShowSnriTracking": "Show SNRI tracking",
  "settingsNotifySnri": "Notify SNRI quitting progress",
  "addictionTca": "TCAs",
  "@addictionTca": {
    "description": "Tricyclic antidepressant addiction name"
