  "tcaPageTitle": "TCA Recovery",
  "tcaHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "tcaHeaderNotStarted": "Benefits of Tapering Off TCAs",
  "tcaSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone",
  "tcaSubtitleNotStarted": "See what happens when you taper off",
  "tcaMilestone3Title": "Day three: go carefully",
  "tcaMilestone3Description": "Stopping a tricyclic antidepressant can bring nausea, insomnia, anxiety or dizziness, especially after a fast taper. Tell your prescriber if symptoms are difficult.",
  "tcaMilestone7Title": "One week after the taper",
  "tcaMilestone7Description": "Seven days. Withdrawal symptoms may still be active, and that's not unusual. Medical support can help you work through them.",
  "tcaMilestone14Title": "Two weeks, taking stock",
  "tcaMilestone14Description": "Two weeks after stopping. Early symptoms often ease around this period, though some persist much longer. Check how you're really doing.",
  "tcaMilestone30Title": "A month after stopping",
  "tcaMilestone30Description": "Thirty days since your TCA taper. Some people are past the acute symptoms; others need more time and care.",
  "tcaMilestone90Title": "Three months on",
  "tcaMilestone90Description": "Three months. That's a substantial stretch after discontinuation, even if mood, sleep or other symptoms still need attention.",
  "tcaMilestone180Title": "Six months, looking after you",
  "tcaMilestone180Description": "Half a year after stopping. Studies across antidepressants find many people remain well, but relapse is possible. Keep your support in place.",
  "tcaMilestone365Title": "A year since the taper",
  "tcaMilestone365Description": "A full year. Research across antidepressants shows some people stay well after stopping and others relapse. If you're doing well, take the win.",
  "tcaMilestone730Title": "Two years",
  "tcaMilestone730Description": "Two years since stopping your TCA. How you're feeling matters more than the date.",
  "tcaReferenceDay730": "Two Years After TCAs: Sustained Progress\n\nSource: NICE NG222 — Depression in adults: treatment and management\n\nNICE states that withdrawal symptoms can occur with tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs), that experiences vary between people, and that dose reduction should be gradual and individually paced. Difficult withdrawal can last longer than the usual early window.\n\nNICE does not define a two-year biological recovery threshold after stopping a TCA. This anniversary celebrates two years of sustained progress without assigning a universal finish line to recovery.",
  "tcaMilestone1095Title": "Three years, still going",
  "tcaMilestone1095Description": "Three years. Whatever support and treatment decisions have helped, they've got you this far.",
  "tcaReferenceDay1095": "Three Years After TCAs: Progress That Holds\n\nSource: NICE NG222 — Depression in adults: treatment and management\n\nNICE recommends that antidepressant withdrawal be tailored to the person, taking account of the medicine, treatment duration, symptoms, and response to each dose reduction. TCAs are explicitly among the antidepressants that can cause withdrawal symptoms.\n\nThe guideline does not identify three years as a universal TCA recovery deadline. This milestone recognises the stability, routines, and progress maintained across three years.",
  "tcaMilestone1460Title": "Four years, your pace",
  "tcaMilestone1460Description": "Four years after the taper. Recovery takes different amounts of time for different people. Keep using the care that helps you stay well.",
  "tcaReferenceDay1460": "Four Years After TCAs: Long-Term Progress\n\nSource: NICE NG222 — Depression in adults: treatment and management\n\nNICE describes antidepressant withdrawal as variable rather than governed by one fixed timetable. It recommends gradual dose reductions and adjusting the pace to the individual's symptoms and circumstances.\n\nThere is no evidence-based four-year biological threshold in the guideline. Four years is still a meaningful marker of long-term progress and of the ground held over time.",
  "tcaMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "tcaMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade since stopping your TCA. If this remains the right treatment decision for you, five years is a major anniversary.",
  "tcaReferenceDay1825": "Five Years After TCAs: A Major Anniversary\n\nSource: NICE NG222 — Depression in adults: treatment and management\n\nNICE guidance focuses on safe, individualised withdrawal and monitoring rather than assigning one recovery schedule to everyone who stops an antidepressant. TCAs are among the medicines covered by that guidance.\n\nThe evidence does not create a five-year TCA recovery threshold. Five years remains a major personal anniversary and a strong marker of sustained change.",
  "settingsShowTcaTracking": "Show TCA tracking",
  "settingsNotifyTca": "Notify TCA quitting progress",
