  "maoiMilestone3Title": "Day three: medical support",
  "maoiMilestone3Description": "MAOI discontinuation can be serious. Agitation, confusion, fever or unusual muscle jerks need urgent medical attention. Follow a prescriber-led plan.",
  "maoiMilestone7Title": "One week, still cautious",
  "maoiMilestone7Description": "Seven days after stopping. MAO enzyme activity is recovering, but drug interactions and withdrawal risks may still matter. Stick with medical advice.",
  "maoiMilestone14Title": "Two weeks: check the plan",
  "maoiMilestone14Description": "Around two weeks is an important point for many irreversible MAOIs, but interaction precautions depend on the drug. Only change food or medicine restrictions with your prescriber.",
  "maoiMilestone30Title": "A month after stopping",
  "maoiMilestone30Description": "Thirty days since stopping your MAOI. The early interaction window may have passed, but mood and withdrawal symptoms still deserve attention.",
  "maoiMilestone90Title": "Three months, check in",
  "maoiMilestone90Description": "Three months since stopping. Check how you're sleeping, coping and feeling, and get support where you need it.",
  "maoiMilestone180Title": "Half a year on",
  "maoiMilestone180Description": "Six months after your MAOI taper. That's a substantial stretch of managing treatment and wellbeing. Keep using whatever support works.",
  "maoiMilestone365Title": "One year after the taper",
  "maoiMilestone365Description": "A full year since stopping. If you're doing well, that's significant. Keep your mental-health care and follow-up on your own terms.",
