  "vapingDay1Description": "Day one without the vape. If your vape contained nicotine, cravings, irritability, restlessness, low mood, poor sleep or fuzzy focus can be nicotine withdrawal — not a reason to feed it. Either way, you’re already breaking the vaping loop.",
  "@vapingDay1Description": {
    "description": "Vaping milestone day 1 evidence-based description"
  },
  "vapingDay3Description": "Three days vape-free. In a clinical study of former smokers who vaped daily, mean withdrawal peaked on day 1 or 2, then declined across the next four abstinent days. You’re now beyond that study’s early peak window; cravings can still show up, but they don’t own the controls.",
  "@vapingDay3Description": {
    "description": "Vaping milestone day 3 evidence-based description"
  },
  "vapingDay7Description": "Seven full days without inhaling vape aerosol. That’s a week with no new vaping exposure to nicotine, ultrafine particles, heavy metals, volatile organic compounds or other harmful substances the aerosol can carry. Clear streak — keep it rolling.",
  "@vapingDay7Description": {
    "description": "Vaping milestone day 7 evidence-based description"
  },
  "vapingLongStreakDescription": "{days} days without taking another hit of vape aerosol. That’s {days} days with no new vaping exposure to the harmful substances that aerosol can carry. If your vape contained nicotine, it’s also {days} days without reinforcing that dependence. Huge streak — keep it moving.",
  "@vapingLongStreakDescription": {
    "description": "Vaping long-streak evidence-based description",
    "placeholders": {
      "days": {
        "type": "int"
      }
    }
  },
  "vapingMilestone730Title": "Two years vape-free",
  "vapingMilestone730Description": "Two years without a vape. You've gone 730 days without reinforcing that nicotine habit.",
  "vapingMilestone1095Title": "Three years",
  "vapingMilestone1095Description": "Three years vape-free. The long-term health picture is still being studied, but three years without going back speaks for itself.",
  "vapingMilestone1460Title": "Four years, still clear",
  "vapingMilestone1460Description": "Four years without vaping. The habit hasn't had a place in your day for a long time. Nice work keeping it that way.",
  "vapingMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "vapingMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade vape-free. You've kept vaping out of your life for five years.",
  "vapingLongTermReference": "Vaping, Quitting, and Long-Term Evidence\n\nSource: CDC — Vaping and Quitting; CDC — Health Effects of Vaping\n\nCDC says people who vape can become addicted to nicotine, quitting may cause nicotine-withdrawal symptoms, and scientists are still studying the best ways to help people quit vaping. CDC also says the long-term health effects of e-cigarettes are still being studied.\n\nThese sources do not define 2-, 3-, 4-, or 5-year biological recovery thresholds after quitting vaping. Quitter therefore treats these dates as anniversaries of sustained vape-free progress, not medical endpoints.",
  "vapingWithdrawalReference": "E-cigarette abstinence and withdrawal\n\nSource: Hughes et al., Nicotine & Tobacco Research (2020)\n\nIn 109 former smokers who were daily e-cigarette users, six days of biologically confirmed abstinence increased nicotine-withdrawal symptoms and cravings. Symptoms followed the classic rise-and-fall pattern of withdrawal, with a strong increase during the first two abstinent days. The study supports real e-cigarette withdrawal; it does not establish a universal day-by-day recovery clock.",
  "@vapingWithdrawalReference": {
    "description": "Localized reference summary for e-cigarette abstinence and withdrawal"
  },
  "vapingHealthReference": "What vaping exposes you to\n\nSource: CDC — Health Effects of Vaping\n\nCDC says most e-cigarettes contain addictive nicotine. Vape aerosol can contain nicotine, cancer-causing chemicals, heavy metals such as nickel, tin and lead, tiny particles that reach deep into the lungs, volatile organic compounds, and some harmful flavoring chemicals. Nicotine withdrawal can include irritability, anxiety or restlessness, low mood, sleep and concentration problems, hunger and cravings, and these symptoms fade over time. Long-term vaping effects are still being studied, so Quitter celebrates sustained abstinence instead of borrowing cigarette carbon-monoxide, tar, cancer or heart-risk timelines.",
  "@vapingHealthReference": {
    "description": "Localized reference summary for CDC vaping health effects"
  },
  "adderallMilestone730Title": "Two years without misuse",
  "adderallMilestone730Description": "Two years. You've kept Adderall misuse out of the picture while life carried on.",
  "adderallReferenceDay730": "Two Years Off Adderall: Durable Progress\n\nSource: SAMHSA, Treatment of Stimulant Use Disorders\n\nSAMHSA’s guide covers amphetamine-type stimulants, including prescription stimulant misuse, and supports evidence-based care for recovery. It does not define a two-year biological reset.\n\nTwo years is still a major achievement. You have maintained change over a long stretch; celebrate the stability you’ve built and keep using what works.",
  "adderallMilestone1095Title": "Three years of progress",
  "adderallMilestone1095Description": "Three years. The habits and support you've built have been tested by plenty of real life.",
  "adderallReferenceDay1095": "Three Years Off Adderall: Recovery With Momentum\n\nSource: SAMHSA, Treatment of Stimulant Use Disorders\n\nSAMHSA supports evidence-based treatment and long-term recovery from stimulant use disorders, including problems involving prescription stimulants. The guide does not claim that a particular brain change finishes at three years.\n\nThree years reflects durable change carried through ordinary days as well as difficult ones. Keep the routines, relationships, and support that helped you reach this point.",
  "adderallMilestone1460Title": "Four years, still clear",
  "adderallMilestone1460Description": "Four years without misuse. You've kept making this choice through changes in motivation and everyday life.",
  "adderallReferenceDay1460": "Four Years Off Adderall: Long-Term Change in Practice\n\nSource: SAMHSA, Treatment of Stimulant Use Disorders\n\nSAMHSA’s guide focuses on treatments and supports that help people achieve long-term recovery. It does not define a four-year medical finish line, so this milestone is about sustained progress rather than an invented health promise.\n\nFour years shows consistency on a genuinely long timescale. Protect the structures and people that keep your progress steady.",
  "adderallMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "adderallMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade without misusing Adderall. Five years of protecting the life you've built.",
  "adderallReferenceDay1825": "Five Years Off Adderall: Half a Decade of Progress\n\nSource: SAMHSA, Treatment of Stimulant Use Disorders\n\nSAMHSA recognizes long-term recovery as an important goal of stimulant-use-disorder care. Its evidence guide does not promise a special biological event at five years, and this milestone does not need one to matter.\n\nFive years is 1,825 days of maintaining a different direction. That is a substantial body of lived evidence that your change can endure. Celebrate it, then keep doing the things that made it possible.",
  "alcoholMilestone730Title": "Two years",
  "alcoholMilestone730Description": "Two full years without alcohol. Holidays, stress and ordinary Tuesdays, handled without a drink. Better sleep and blood pressure may be among the gains.",
  "alcoholReferenceDay730": "Sustained Change\n\nTwo years alcohol-free represents sustained behavioural change. The benefits extend beyond removing alcohol itself: daily routines, relationships, physical health and psychological well-being are all important parts of long-term recovery.",
  "alcoholMilestone1095Title": "Three years, still clear",
  "alcoholMilestone1095Description": "Three years alcohol-free. That's a long time to build a life without drinking. Risks for some alcohol-related cancers also keep falling after quitting.",
  "alcoholReferenceDay1095": "A Different Normal\n\nThree years of abstinence demonstrates consistency across a substantial period of life. Alcohol is no longer part of your daily routine, and the habits supporting that change have been reinforced over thousands of decisions.",
  "alcoholMilestone1460Title": "Four years",
  "alcoholMilestone1460Description": "Four years without a drink. Whatever the world threw your way, you've kept alcohol out of it. The health benefits of staying off it keep adding up.",
  "alcoholReferenceDay1460": "Long-Term Progress\n\nFour years without alcohol represents a major period without repeated alcohol-related exposure. Long-term recovery is built through consistent behaviour, supported by healthier routines and meaningful improvements in everyday life.",
  "alcoholMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "alcoholMilestone1825Description": "Five years without alcohol, through birthdays, bad days and everything between. The long-term health benefits keep adding up.",
  "alcoholReferenceDay1825": "Five Years of Freedom\n\nHalf a decade without drinking is a significant achievement in its own right. Five years of sustained abstinence means five years without alcohol continuing to damage your health. It is a milestone worth recognising and celebrating.",
  "ghbMilestone730Title": "Two years",
  "ghbMilestone730Description": "Two years without GHB. You've kept the change through more than just the difficult early days.",
  "ghbReferenceDay730": "Two Years Without GHB: Durable Progress\n\nSource: SAMHSA, Recovery and Recovery Support\n\nSAMHSA describes recovery as an ongoing, individual process of change. It does not set a GHB-specific biological endpoint at any of these anniversaries.\n\nTwo years is 730 days of maintaining a different direction. Celebrate the durability and keep the people, routines, and support that make it sustainable.",
  "ghbMilestone1095Title": "Three years clear",
  "ghbMilestone1095Description": "Three years without GHB. Keeping this change going for that long is worth celebrating.",
  "ghbReferenceDay1095": "Three Years Without GHB: Stronger Ground\n\nSource: SAMHSA, Recovery and Recovery Support\n\nSAMHSA describes recovery as an ongoing, individual process of change. It does not set a GHB-specific biological endpoint at any of these anniversaries.\n\nThree years is 1,095 days of maintained change. That is a substantial record of stability and of protecting what helps recovery continue.",
  "ghbMilestone1460Title": "Four years of follow-through",
  "ghbMilestone1460Description": "Four years without GHB. The routines and support behind that choice have carried you a long way.",
  "ghbReferenceDay1460": "Four Years Without GHB: Lasting Change\n\nSource: SAMHSA, Recovery and Recovery Support\n\nSAMHSA describes recovery as an ongoing, individual process of change. It does not set a GHB-specific biological endpoint at any of these anniversaries.\n\nFour years is 1,460 days of carrying change through ordinary life. The durability itself deserves recognition, along with the support and habits behind it.",
  "ghbMilestone1825Title": "Five years without GHB",
  "ghbMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade. Five years of sticking with the change, with all the normal messiness of life along the way. That's huge.",
  "ghbReferenceDay1825": "Five Years Without GHB: Half a Decade\n\nSource: SAMHSA, Recovery and Recovery Support\n\nSAMHSA describes recovery as an ongoing, individual process of change. It does not set a GHB-specific biological endpoint at any of these anniversaries.\n\nFive years is 1,825 days of maintained change: half a decade of repeatedly choosing a different direction. That scale of commitment deserves real credit.",
  "inhalantsMilestone1095Title": "Three years clear",
  "inhalantsMilestone1095Description": "Three years without inhalants. You've avoided renewed exposure throughout that time.",
  "inhalantsMilestone1460Title": "Four years of staying clear",
  "inhalantsMilestone1460Description": "Four years away from inhalants. You've stuck with the change through a lot of ordinary life.",
  "inhalantsMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "inhalantsMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade without inhalants. Recovery can be complicated, but five years without renewed exposure is a major achievement.",
  "inhalantsReferenceDay1095": "Three Years Off Inhalants\n\nSource: Cairney et al., \"A prospective study of neurocognitive changes 15 years after chronic inhalant abuse,\" Addiction (2013), on PubMed\n\nBeyond Two Years\nThe study found substantial recovery after two years of abstinence in participants without lead encephalopathy and followed the same cohort to 15 years. It did not identify a special three-year biological threshold.\n\nThree Years of Progress\nThree years marks sustained abstinence. Long-term recovery can continue, while outcomes depend on prior exposure and injury; lead encephalopathy may leave persistent deficits.",
  "inhalantsReferenceDay1460": "Four Years Off Inhalants\n\nSource: Cairney et al., \"A prospective study of neurocognitive changes 15 years after chronic inhalant abuse,\" Addiction (2013), on PubMed\n\nRecovery Over the Long Term\nMost measured scores had normalised by two years in the group without lead encephalopathy, and the cohort was assessed again at 15 years. The paper does not assign a distinct recovery event to year four.\n\nFour Years of Distance\nFour years adds another full year of sustained abstinence and a strong recovery foundation. Outcomes vary, especially when prior inhalant exposure caused severe neurological injury.",
  "inhalantsReferenceDay1825": "Five Years Off Inhalants\n\nSource: Cairney et al., \"A prospective study of neurocognitive changes 15 years after chronic inhalant abuse,\" Addiction (2013), on PubMed\n\nA Long-Term Milestone\nThe study supports the possibility of substantial neurological and cognitive recovery with long-term abstinence when lead encephalopathy is absent. Its measured checkpoints were two and 15 years, so it does not claim that five years is a universal biological finish line.\n\nFive Years Built\nFive years is durable progress: a large span of life maintained away from inhalants. The evidence supports long-term recovery while recognising that severe lead-related injury can remain persistent."
,
  "maoiMilestone730Title": "Two years",
  "maoiMilestone730Description": "Two years after your MAOI taper. You've spent that time managing your wellbeing.",
  "maoiMilestone1095Title": "Three years, steady",
  "maoiMilestone1095Description": "Three years. You've had another full year to learn what keeps you well.",
  "maoiMilestone1460Title": "Four years, still you",
  "maoiMilestone1460Description": "Four years after stopping. Recovery isn't one fixed schedule, and continued treatment is never a failure. Recognise your own progress.",
  "maoiMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "maoiMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade since stopping your MAOI. If this has been the right path for you, it's a big anniversary.",
  "maoiReferenceDay730": "Two Years After MAOIs\n\nSource: Gahr et al. (2013), systematic review of tranylcypromine withdrawal and discontinuation.\n\nThe review documents potentially severe syndromes after abrupt tranylcypromine cessation, but it does not establish a special biological recovery threshold at two years. This anniversary therefore marks sustained change, support, and routines rather than a medical finish line.",
  "maoiReferenceDay1095": "Three Years After MAOIs\n\nSource: Gahr et al. (2013), systematic review of tranylcypromine withdrawal and discontinuation.\n\nThe review characterises acute withdrawal and discontinuation after tranylcypromine, but it does not define a three-year physiological recovery point. Three years celebrates another full year of maintaining the direction you chose and the support that helped make it durable.",
  "maoiReferenceDay1460": "Four Years After MAOIs\n\nSource: Gahr et al. (2013), systematic review of tranylcypromine withdrawal and discontinuation.\n\nPublished MAOI discontinuation evidence does not identify a universal four-year biological endpoint. Four years instead reflects sustained follow-through across changing seasons, stresses, and routines, which is worth celebrating without inventing a clinical deadline.",
  "maoiReferenceDay1825": "Five Years After MAOIs\n\nSource: Gahr et al. (2013), systematic review of tranylcypromine withdrawal and discontinuation.\n\nThe MAOI withdrawal literature addresses discontinuation risk and safe stopping; it does not define five years as a cure date or universal biological finish line. Five years is still a major milestone because of the time, habits, and support sustained across 1,825 days.",
  "marijuanaMilestone730Title": "Two years",
  "marijuanaMilestone730Description": "Two years without cannabis. Recovery isn't one-size-fits-all. Keeping this going for two years is worth celebrating all by itself.",
  "marijuanaReferenceDay730": "Two Years Without Cannabis\n\nSource: SAMHSA (2026), Addressing Cannabis Use Disorder in Primary Care Settings—A Lifespan Approach.\n\nSAMHSA says cannabis use disorder varies by person and life stage and calls for tailored, evidence-based care. It does not define a universal biological recovery threshold at two years.\n\nTwo years therefore marks sustained change, routines, and support, not a medical finish line.",
  "marijuanaMilestone1095Title": "Three years, still clear",
  "marijuanaMilestone1095Description": "Three years. The habits and people that support your recovery have had a lot of time to become part of normal life.",
  "marijuanaReferenceDay1095": "Three Years Without Cannabis\n\nSource: SAMHSA (2026), Addressing Cannabis Use Disorder in Primary Care Settings—A Lifespan Approach.\n\nThe advisory emphasizes that cannabis-related risks, symptoms, and treatment needs differ across people and stages of life. It does not identify a universal three-year biological endpoint.\n\nThree years celebrates durable choices and the support that helped those choices keep holding.",
  "marijuanaMilestone1460Title": "Four years",
  "marijuanaMilestone1460Description": "Four years without cannabis. Life changes, stress happens, and you've kept this part steady.",
  "marijuanaReferenceDay1460": "Four Years Without Cannabis\n\nSource: SAMHSA (2026), Addressing Cannabis Use Disorder in Primary Care Settings—A Lifespan Approach.\n\nSAMHSA recommends individualized, evidence-based care because cannabis use disorder does not follow one identical course. The advisory does not set a universal four-year recovery threshold.\n\nFour years reflects continuity through changing routines, stresses, and seasons. That consistency is the milestone.",
  "marijuanaMilestone1825Title": "Five years without cannabis",
  "marijuanaMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade without cannabis. You've spent five years living without the old habit.",
  "marijuanaReferenceDay1825": "Five Years Without Cannabis\n\nSource: SAMHSA (2026), Addressing Cannabis Use Disorder in Primary Care Settings—A Lifespan Approach.\n\nThe SAMHSA advisory supports tailored care across the lifespan and does not define a five-year cure date or universal biological finish line for cannabis use disorder.\n\nFive years is a major long-term achievement: sustained direction, practiced routines, and support carried across half a decade.",
  "mdmaMilestone730Title": "Two years",
  "mdmaMilestone730Description": "Two years without MDMA. Keeping this change going is worth celebrating.",
  "mdmaReferenceDay730": "Two Years Off MDMA\n\nSource: Ung et al. (2026), systematic review and meta-analysis of long-term neurocognitive outcomes after sustained MDMA abstinence.\n\nThe review included studies with at least six months of abstinence, judged the evidence low quality, and did not find that longer abstinence reliably predicted better learning and memory outcomes. It does not establish two years as a universal biological recovery point.\n\nTwo years is still a substantial marker of sustained change, built through repeated choices rather than a medical finish line.",
  "mdmaMilestone1095Title": "Three years, no comeback",
  "mdmaMilestone1095Description": "Three years away from MDMA. The habits and choices behind that have held for another whole year.",
  "mdmaReferenceDay1095": "Three Years Off MDMA\n\nSource: Ung et al. (2026), systematic review and meta-analysis of long-term neurocognitive outcomes after sustained MDMA abstinence.\n\nAvailable studies do not define a three-year point when cognitive effects universally resolve. The review found no reliable relationship between longer abstinence and greater learning or memory recovery, and rated the evidence low quality.\n\nThree years therefore celebrates durable momentum and consistency without claiming an unsupported biological deadline.",
  "mdmaMilestone1460Title": "Four years of follow-through",
  "mdmaMilestone1460Description": "Four years. Whatever else changed in your life, you kept this part steady.",
  "mdmaReferenceDay1460": "Four Years Off MDMA\n\nSource: Ung et al. (2026), systematic review and meta-analysis of long-term neurocognitive outcomes after sustained MDMA abstinence.\n\nResearch after sustained abstinence remains limited and does not establish four years as a universal recovery threshold. Some measured differences may persist, while the review cautions that the underlying evidence is low quality.\n\nFour years marks deeply established change across changing routines, stresses, and seasons. That continuity is the achievement.",
  "mdmaMilestone1825Title": "Five years without MDMA",
  "mdmaMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade without MDMA. You've kept this change going for five years.",
  "mdmaReferenceDay1825": "Five Years Off MDMA\n\nSource: Ung et al. (2026), systematic review and meta-analysis of long-term neurocognitive outcomes after sustained MDMA abstinence.\n\nThe current evidence does not support treating five years as a cure date or universal neurocognitive finish line. The review found limited, low-quality evidence and no dependable pattern of greater learning or memory recovery with longer abstinence.\n\nFive years remains a major long-term milestone: half a decade of maintained direction deserves recognition on its own terms.",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone730Title": "Two years, no return",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone730Description": "Two years without nitrous. There's no fixed nerve-repair timetable, and some symptoms persist. You've kept further exposure out of the picture.",
  "nitrousOxideReferenceDay730": "Two Years - Progress Holds\n\nSource: Marsden, Sharma & Rotella (2022), systematic review of chronic nitrous oxide misuse.\n\nThe review found neurological problems were characteristic of chronic nitrous oxide misuse, vitamin B12 replacement was the main treatment, and partial recovery was the most commonly reported outcome. It did not define a universal multi-year recovery timetable.\n\nTwo years without nitrous oxide is 730 days without renewed exposure. Evidence shows neurological outcomes vary and partial recovery is commonly reported after cessation and treatment, so this milestone celebrates sustained protection rather than a fixed medical endpoint.",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone1095Title": "Three years of distance",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone1095Description": "Three years without nitrous. You've avoided further exposure throughout that time.",
  "nitrousOxideReferenceDay1095": "Three Years - Stronger Ground\n\nSource: Marsden, Sharma & Rotella (2022), systematic review of chronic nitrous oxide misuse.\n\nThe review found neurological problems were characteristic of chronic nitrous oxide misuse, vitamin B12 replacement was the main treatment, and partial recovery was the most commonly reported outcome. It did not define a universal multi-year recovery timetable.\n\nThree years is a long stretch without restarting the exposure that caused the risk. Research does not map nerve recovery to this anniversary; the achievement is maintaining the change for 1095 days.",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone1460Title": "Four years",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone1460Description": "Four years without nitrous oxide. Whatever recovery has involved, you've stayed with the change for another full year.",
  "nitrousOxideReferenceDay1460": "Four Years - Built to Last\n\nSource: Marsden, Sharma & Rotella (2022), systematic review of chronic nitrous oxide misuse.\n\nThe review found neurological problems were characteristic of chronic nitrous oxide misuse, vitamin B12 replacement was the main treatment, and partial recovery was the most commonly reported outcome. It did not define a universal multi-year recovery timetable.\n\nFour years means another full year of protecting the progress already made. Nitrous oxide studies report variable recovery and persistent deficits in some cases, not a universal four-year finish line.",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "nitrousOxideMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade without nitrous. Five years of keeping a risky habit out of your life.",
  "nitrousOxideReferenceDay1825": "Five Years - Lasting Change\n\nSource: Marsden, Sharma & Rotella (2022), systematic review of chronic nitrous oxide misuse.\n\nThe review found neurological problems were characteristic of chronic nitrous oxide misuse, vitamin B12 replacement was the main treatment, and partial recovery was the most commonly reported outcome. It did not define a universal multi-year recovery timetable.\n\nFive years without nitrous oxide is half a decade of sustained change. There is no evidence-based five-year neurological reset date; 1825 days of maintained direction is the milestone.",
  "socialMediaMilestone730Title": "Two years of your time",
  "socialMediaMilestone730Description": "Two years with more control over your attention. The routine has stuck long past the novelty of quitting.",
  "socialMediaReferenceDay730": "Two Years With Social Media in Its Place\n\nSource: Ramadhan et al., “Impacts of digital social media detox for mental health: A systematic review and meta-analysis,” Narra J (2024), PubMed PMID 39280291\n\nThe meta-analysis combined 10 studies of digital-detox interventions. Depressive symptoms improved overall; pooled effects on life satisfaction, stress, and overall mental well-being were not statistically significant.\n\nThe included interventions do not establish a two-year health threshold. Day 730 celebrates two years of sustained, intentional digital choices, not a biological reset deadline.",
  "socialMediaMilestone1095Title": "Three years",
  "socialMediaMilestone1095Description": "Three years of keeping social media in its place. Your time has been yours to spend.",
  "socialMediaReferenceDay1095": "Three Years: Attention on Your Terms\n\nSource: Ramadhan et al., “Impacts of digital social media detox for mental health: A systematic review and meta-analysis,” Narra J (2024), PubMed PMID 39280291\n\nThe meta-analysis combined 10 studies of digital-detox interventions. Depressive symptoms improved overall; pooled effects on life satisfaction, stress, and overall mental well-being were not statistically significant.\n\nThe research does not define 1,095 days as a mental-health or neurological endpoint. This milestone celebrates three years of keeping your digital habits deliberate.",
  "socialMediaMilestone1460Title": "Four years, feed optional",
  "socialMediaMilestone1460Description": "Four years. Algorithms can keep arguing for your attention; you don't have to answer.",
  "socialMediaReferenceDay1460": "Four Years: Deliberate by Default\n\nSource: Ramadhan et al., “Impacts of digital social media detox for mental health: A systematic review and meta-analysis,” Narra J (2024), PubMed PMID 39280291\n\nThe meta-analysis combined 10 studies of digital-detox interventions. Depressive symptoms improved overall; pooled effects on life satisfaction, stress, and overall mental well-being were not statistically significant.\n\nThe review does not test a four-year abstinence or reduction milestone. Day 1,460 is a behavioural anniversary: four years of maintaining a healthier boundary through ordinary life.",
  "socialMediaMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "socialMediaMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade without the old scrolling habit. Five years of choosing what deserves your attention.",
  "socialMediaReferenceDay1825": "Five Years: Life Beyond the Feed\n\nSource: Ramadhan et al., “Impacts of digital social media detox for mental health: A systematic review and meta-analysis,” Narra J (2024), PubMed PMID 39280291\n\nThe meta-analysis combined 10 studies of digital-detox interventions. Depressive symptoms improved overall; pooled effects on life satisfaction, stress, and overall mental well-being were not statistically significant.\n\nThe review does not establish five-year outcomes. Day 1,825 is worth celebrating for what it actually is: half a decade of intentional digital choices, without an invented brain-reset timeline.",
  "ssriMilestone730Title": "Two years of progress",
  "ssriMilestone730Description": "Two years since your SSRI taper. There's no universal recovery clock. Whatever care and routines have helped you, you've maintained them for a long time.",
  "ssriReferenceDay730": "Two Years After SSRIs: Steady Progress\n\nSource: Royal College of Psychiatrists — Stopping antidepressants\n\nThe Royal College says antidepressants should not usually be stopped suddenly and that tapering should be adapted to the individual. Withdrawal experiences vary, and some people need slower reductions over months or longer.\n\nThe guidance does not define a two-year biological recovery threshold after stopping an SSRI. Day 730 celebrates two years of sustained progress, not a fixed neurological timetable.",
  "ssriMilestone1095Title": "Three years, doing well",
  "ssriMilestone1095Description": "Three years since stopping. Medication choices are personal, and continued support is sensible.",
  "ssriReferenceDay1095": "Three Years After SSRIs: Progress Sustained\n\nSource: Royal College of Psychiatrists — Stopping antidepressants\n\nThe guidance describes withdrawal as individual: tapering pace depends on factors such as how long the medicine was taken and whether previous dose reductions caused symptoms.\n\nIt does not establish three years as a universal SSRI recovery deadline. Day 1095 recognises progress maintained over three years without inventing a clinical threshold.",
  "ssriMilestone1460Title": "Four years",
  "ssriMilestone1460Description": "Four years since stopping your SSRI. Recognise your own progress and keep using the treatment and support that help you stay well.",
  "ssriReferenceDay1460": "Four Years After SSRIs: Stronger Ground\n\nSource: Royal College of Psychiatrists — Stopping antidepressants\n\nThe Royal College recommends gradual, individualised tapering because withdrawal severity and duration differ between people. Some people need reductions spread across months or longer.\n\nThat evidence does not create a four-year neurological endpoint. Day 1460 celebrates four years of sustained change and the stability built along the way.",
  "ssriMilestone1825Title": "Five years",
  "ssriMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade after stopping your SSRI. If this remains the right approach for you, five years of looking after your wellbeing is worth celebrating.",
  "ssriReferenceDay1825": "Five Years After SSRIs: Major Milestone\n\nSource: Royal College of Psychiatrists — Stopping antidepressants\n\nThe guidance focuses on safe, individualised tapering and responding to symptoms rather than assigning one fixed recovery schedule to everyone.\n\nThere is no evidence-based five-year SSRI recovery threshold in that guidance. Day 1825 is still a major personal anniversary: half a decade of sustained progress.",
  "steroidsMilestone1095Title": "Three years",
  "steroidsMilestone1095Description": "Three years steroid-free. Hormone recovery varies, and you've given yourself three years without another cycle.",
  "steroidsReferenceDay1095": "Three Years Off Anabolic Steroids\n\nSource: Physical, psychological and biochemical recovery from anabolic steroid-induced hypogonadism: a scoping review, Endocrine Connections (2023)\n\nThree Years of Distance\nRecovery after AAS is highly variable. The review reports testosterone and gonadotropin recovery over months, while testicular size and sperm production may recover over months to years. Psychological recovery is less predictable.\n\nWhat This Anniversary Means\nThe review does not define a three-year biological finish line. This milestone marks three years without re-exposure. Persistent low-testosterone, fertility, mood, or sexual symptoms are reasons to seek medical assessment.",
  "steroidsMilestone1460Title": "Four years, no return",
  "steroidsMilestone1460Description": "Four years without another anabolic-steroid cycle. You've kept this change going through everyday life.",
  "steroidsReferenceDay1460": "Four Years Off Anabolic Steroids\n\nSource: Physical, psychological and biochemical recovery from anabolic steroid-induced hypogonadism: a scoping review, Endocrine Connections (2023)\n\nFour Years of Consistency\nRecovery after AAS does not follow one clock. The review describes physical, reproductive, psychological, and biochemical recovery on different timelines, with some changes taking months to years.\n\nNo Invented Deadline\nThere is no evidence-based four-year recovery threshold. Four years without another cycle is still a substantial achievement. Ongoing symptoms deserve clinical assessment on their own merits.",
  "steroidsMilestone1825Title": "Five years off steroids",
  "steroidsMilestone1825Description": "Half a decade without another cycle. Recovery isn't always neat, but five years of protecting your health deserves a proper celebration.",
  "steroidsReferenceDay1825": "Five Years Off Anabolic Steroids\n\nSource: Physical, psychological and biochemical recovery from anabolic steroid-induced hypogonadism: a scoping review, Endocrine Connections (2023)\n\nFive Years Is a Major Milestone\nThe evidence shows that recovery after AAS varies widely. Many hormonal changes improve much earlier, while reproductive or psychological effects can follow longer and less predictable courses.\n\nHalf a Decade Without Re-Exposure\nThe review does not set a five-year biological finish line. Five years steroid-free is worth celebrating for the sustained change itself, while any persistent symptoms should still be assessed medically."
}
